WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:23.071
Imagine that you’re in charge of sending
a culture-bearing probe off into space; one

00:00:23.071 --> 00:00:27.420
capable of carrying a single example of 20th
Century art.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.140
What would you pick to represent us in the
cosmos?

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.660
Some great novel, perhaps?

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.360
Or would you pick a slender volume based on
a radio show?

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:39.980
A volume emblazoned with the words DON’T
PANIC; a volume that contains everything from

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:45.410
the precise reason you should always carry
a towel, to how to mix the most-powerful drink

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:46.480
in the universe?

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:51.500
We’re talking, of course, about the Hitchhiker’s
Guide to the Galaxy, perhaps one of the most-unique

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:53.239
pieces of sci-fi ever written.

00:00:53.239 --> 00:00:57.690
And the author behind the book we here at
Biographics would send as our emissary to

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.160
the stars?

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.160
Douglas Adams.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:05.680
Born in the 1950s, Adams was the product of
a post-war Britain exploding with creativity.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.180
It was the era of the Beatles and Pink Floyd,
of Monty Python, and Doctor Who.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:16.460
Yet even in this intensely vibrant age, Adams
still managed to dream up a universe so unique

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:19.030
it has arguably never been equalled.

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:24.740
In the video today, we’re thumbing a ride
into the past to explore the life of the man

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:29.119
behind a literary legend.

00:01:29.119 --> 00:01:35.670
Mostly Harmless
Far out in the uncharted backwaters of the

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:41.960
unfashionable end of the western spiral arm
of the Galaxy lies a small unregarded yellow

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:42.960
sun.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:48.770
It was on a planet orbiting this unregarded
sun, in the year 1952, that Douglas Noel Adams

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:49.770
was born.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:53.780
Later in life, Adams liked to joke that he
was the first DNA in Cambridge, having been

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:58.700
born there several months before Watson and
Crick discovered the far more famous version.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:03.850
But aside from raising a smile, that gag may
have also served to hide painful memories.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:08.910
The family Douglas Adams was born into was
both deeply eccentric, and deeply fractured.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:13.020
A lot of this fracturing came courtesy of
the boy’s father, Christopher Douglas Adams.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:17.990
A bombastic giant of a man with a personality
the size of Canada, Christopher was a man

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:20.770
who loved booze, cars, and women.

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:26.180
You’ll notice that’s women, plural, not
woman, singular, which may be why the Adams

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.910
household fairly radiated misery.

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:32.710
Before Adams was even five, his parents had
divorced, and he’d gone to live with his

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:33.910
mother’s parents.

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:36.860
Well, make that “her parents and their animals.”

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:41.720
Adams’s maternal grandmother was an animal
lover par excellence, and the house was overrun

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:47.320
with stray dogs, stray cats, stray badgers,
and anything else that might conceivably eat

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:50.000
your food and poop everywhere.

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.280
Perhaps it was due to the endless clamor in
the house, but Adams became so introverted

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:58.140
at school that his teachers thought him “mentally
subnormal”.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:03.150
But while the boy may have had a weird, unhappy
childhood, he never suffered financially.

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:07.030
Not long after ditching his family, Christopher
Adams had managed to seduce an incredibly

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:10.290
wealthy widow who paid for Adams to have a
private education.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:16.060
Thus it was that, aged 11, the boy was dispatched
to the elite Brentwood boarding school.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:21.010
For Adams, Brentwood was the first time he
ever felt his oddness was appreciated.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:26.900
Not yet 12, Adams was already a 6ft giant,
and would continue to grow until he hit 6ft5.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:31.940
On top of that, he’d inherited both his
father’s gigantic personality and his gigantic

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:36.390
nose, making him both a natural performer
and instantly recognizable.

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:40.370
This translated easily into performing in
school plays, where Adams became renowned

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:42.510
for his bizarre sense of humor.

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:45.490
Not that it was really his sense of humor.

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:50.790
In 1969, the first season of Monty Python’s
Flying Circus had exploded the BBC like a

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:53.400
particularly silly stick of dynamite.

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:56.710
Like everyone else his age, Adams had fallen
in love with the troupe.

00:03:56.710 --> 00:04:01.330
But while most kids wanted to watch the Pythons,
Adams wanted to be them.

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:03.840
As he later commented:
"I wanted to be John Cleese.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:06.810
It took me some time to realise that the job
was taken."

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:10.540
But it wasn’t just Monty Python that had
gripped Adams’s imagination.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:15.490
Britain in 1969 was in the midst of a full-blown
cultural explosion.

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:20.540
There was Python’s comedy, the Beatles’
music, Doctor Who’s sci-fi wanderings.

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:25.640
With a talent as distinct as Adams’s, it’s
tempting to think he just sprang from nowhere.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:31.120
But no, his was a sense of humor born from
a particular moment in British history.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:36.590
But while all the ingredients for his greatest
work were in the air in the 1960s, they only

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:39.140
coalesced together thanks to pure chance.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:43.370
In the summer of 1971, Adams was traveling
around Europe between finishing at Brentwood

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:45.660
and starting at Cambridge University.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:50.710
As the legend goes, he found himself at some
point lying dead drunk in a field in Austria,

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:55.440
contemplating alternately his Hitch-Hiker’s
Guide to Europe and the stars spinning above

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:56.440
him.

00:04:56.440 --> 00:05:00.070
Just before he lost consciousness, the drunken
teenager had a thought that would change his

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:01.070
life.

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:04.520
He decided that someone should write a hitchhiker’s
guide not to another continent…

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:06.610
...but to the galaxy.

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:14.700
The Python Years
If it was overindulgence in alcohol that gave

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:20.130
Adams his great idea, it was likely also alcohol
that stopped him from doing anything with

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:21.130
it.

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:26.250
By 1974, Adams had graduated Cambridge after
student years in which he did heroically little

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:29.660
work and heroically huge amounts of drinking.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:34.200
Now he was living in London, still drinking
heroic amounts, but doing nothing except writing

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:38.030
the odd sketch the BBC would immediately reject.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:42.120
But while the boozy London life was keeping
Adams from his art, it was also gonna give

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:43.750
him his bug break.

00:05:43.750 --> 00:05:46.580
That same year, Adams was introduced to Graham
Chapman.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:50.680
If that name sounds familiar, it’s because
Chapman was the fabled straight man of Monty

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:55.420
Python - straight in the comedic sense, as
Chapman was openly gay.

00:05:55.420 --> 00:06:00.720
He was also a man who liked to drink, and
liked others who liked to drink.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:04.240
And in Adams, he seemed to have finally found
the perfect drinking buddy.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:08.750
Throughout the year, the two met up to imbibe
indescribable quantities of alcohol.

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:13.240
The more often they met, the more Chapman
became convinced Adams had a talent for sketch

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:14.240
comedy.

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:18.000
So he gave him a spot on the biggest sketch
show on Earth.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:23.500
On December 5, 1974, Douglas Adams became
one of only two non-members to have ever written

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:24.500
for Monty Python.

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:29.030
His sketch, Patient Abuse, features Chapman
as a doctor who forces a heavily-bleeding

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:33.140
Terry Jones to fill out endless inane forms
before he’ll give him any treatment.

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:38.700
It’s a classic Adams riff on bureaucracy,
albeit one tooled to Python’s specific brand

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:39.700
of humor.

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:43.040
It was also successful enough that Chapman
asked Adams to keep writing with him after

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:44.040
Python ended.

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:47.370
In 1975, the two made a pilot for a sketch
show called Out of the Trees.

00:06:47.370 --> 00:06:49.960
We don’t need to go into Trees too much.

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:54.410
Only one episode was ever made and, as Adams
once said, it “had some good bits, but it

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:56.100
wasn’t really that good.”

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:59.600
Still, it’s worth noting the one big sketch
Adams contributed.

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:03.169
At the end of the episode, a young couple
pick a flower off a bush.

00:07:03.169 --> 00:07:07.600
This causes a chain reaction of events which
begins with the police being called out, progresses

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:12.310
to the whole of Europe going to war, and ends
with the complete destruction of the world.

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:17.240
From that point on, Armageddon would be a
subject that obsessed the young writer.

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:21.540
When Out of the Trees failed, Chapman ditched
his new writing partner, drifting back to

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:23.370
Python’s surreal embrace.

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:28.250
Although Adams would contribute a couple of
one liners to Monty Python and the Holy Grail,

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:31.100
that was really it for his sketch writing
career.

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:33.260
Depressed, Adams returned to his mum’s house.

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:38.670
Over the next year, 1976, he continued to
try his hand at writing and performing, but

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:40.010
it all went nowhere.

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:44.380
There was the sketch show he went on tour
with, which ran at two hours, but only contained

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:45.750
one good gag.

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:49.720
There was the pilot series he wrote with a
friend, about a pair of astronomers who discover

00:07:49.720 --> 00:07:53.380
the world is about to be destroyed to make
way for an interstellar advertising sign,

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:55.210
that the BBC refused to touch.

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:58.630
There was even a spec script he sent to the
Doctor Who production office, one about the

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:03.530
Doctor becoming trapped on a ship crewed entirely
by the useless people of society.

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:07.120
But while the Doctor Who team liked the idea,
they didn’t pick it up.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:11.490
Come Christmas, 1976, Adams was trapped at
his mum’s place, his career dead in the

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:13.640
water, and on the verge of giving up.

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:19.840
At that moment, it must’ve seen to him like
1977 would bring nothing but more disappointment,

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:21.050
more bad news.

00:08:21.050 --> 00:08:25.460
Well, buckle up, because Adams is about to
be proven wrong.

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:29.950
The next twelve months were gonna hit the
young man like a black ship diving into the

00:08:29.950 --> 00:08:33.960
heart of a sun.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:41.070
Hitchhiking the Galaxy
On a dismal February day in 1977, Adams made

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:45.320
a trip to London to have lunch with the BBC’s
light entertainment producer.

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:49.310
It had been over a month since Adams’s depressing
Christmas, and paid writing work was still

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:50.510
out of reach.

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:52.840
That all changed that fateful day.

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:56.690
As the two ate, the producer happened to mention
that he wanted a sci-fi comedy series for

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:57.810
Radio 4.

00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:01.960
He wondered aloud if Adams might like to try
his hand at a pilot episode?

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:04.750
And just like that, Adams was a writer again.

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:09.760
Adams’ initial pitch was The Ends of the
Earth, a six-part comedy in which every episode

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:11.980
would end with the Earth blowing up.

00:09:11.980 --> 00:09:16.420
But as he was writing the pilot - in which
Earth is demolished to make way for a hyperspace

00:09:16.420 --> 00:09:21.820
bypass - Adams decided he needed to give his
alien character, Ford Prefect, a job.

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:27.200
At last, that idea he’d had long ago lying
drunk in a field in Austria came back to him.

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:31.020
Ford Prefect would be a researcher for the
Hitchhiker’s Guide to the Galaxy.

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:36.030
From that moment, the concept of Ends of the
Earth went out the window, replaced by a series

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:41.790
about Ford Prefect, the Guide he was researching,
and his human pal Arthur Dent.

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:45.750
By March, the pilot episode for The Hitchhiker’s
Guide to the Galaxy was ready to go.

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:47.500
The BBC gave it the green light.

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:50.250
That June, the cast assembled in London to
record the episode.

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:53.260
It didn’t take long, and that should’ve
been that.

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:58.360
But something had changed in Douglas Adams,
a zeal had come over him since he’d decided

00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:00.850
what his radio show was gonna be about.

00:10:00.850 --> 00:10:04.080
No longer did Adams simply want to make a
radio comedy.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:08.330
He wanted to make something unlike anything
anyone had ever heard before.

00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:12.690
A sci-fi show with production levels Pink
Floyd would envy.

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:16.540
For the rest of that summer, Adams and his
producer tinkered away in the booth, creating

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:19.110
the sci-fi soundscape for their show.

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:23.830
They spent as long on single sound effects
as some radio shows did on an entire series.

00:10:23.830 --> 00:10:25.620
But it was worth it.

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:28.770
By fall, the pilot was being circulated around
the BBC.

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:29.770
Everyone loved it.

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:33.250
As winter approached, Adams was called in
to two very important meetings.

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:36.890
The first was with the Doctor Who team, who’d
heard his pilot and now wanted him to write

00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:37.890
a four-part episode.

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:40.300
The second was with Radio 4.

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:43.160
They wanted to commission a full series of
Hitchhiker’s.

00:10:43.160 --> 00:10:45.700
Naturally, Adams said yes to both of them.

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:50.460
This would become a pattern in Adams’s life:
taking on so much work he became trapped in

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:54.090
permanent panic mode, barely able to finish
by the deadline.

00:10:54.090 --> 00:10:58.120
In the future, this would become a crippling
problem that severely limited his output.

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:04.140
But in 1977, Adams was still young, and still
just able to get things done.

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:06.060
Although it was touch and go.

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:10.310
The biggest problem was that, while Adams
had a tone and ideas for Hitchhiker’s, he

00:11:10.310 --> 00:11:11.540
didn’t have a story.

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:15.050
So inventing a plot on the fly became harder
and harder.

00:11:15.050 --> 00:11:19.200
Things got so bad that Adams would be forced
to finish episodes inside the booth as the

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:21.029
cast were recording around him.

00:11:21.029 --> 00:11:25.320
When that didn’t work, he had to bring in
his friends to finish them for him.

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:26.520
Still, finish them he did.

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:31.810
At 10:30pm on 8 March, 1978, the first episode
of Hitchhiker’s Guide to the Galaxy was

00:11:31.810 --> 00:11:33.560
broadcast on Radio 4.

00:11:33.560 --> 00:11:38.830
As it went out, Adams presumably clutched
his towel and hoped it would be successful.

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:47.350
But even in his wildest fantasies, he couldn’t
have forseen how successful it would become.

00:11:47.350 --> 00:12:13.089
Doctor Who 
and

00:12:13.089 --> 00:13:06.770
the Writer of Death
Adams would later say of Hitchhiker’s sudden

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:10.930
success that "It was like being helicoptered
to the top of Mount Everest.

00:13:10.930 --> 00:13:13.080
Or having an orgasm without the foreplay."

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:14.080
People loved it.

00:13:14.080 --> 00:13:16.040
Major newspapers raved about it.

00:13:16.040 --> 00:13:19.700
People flooded the Post Office with letters
addressed to the Guide itself.

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:25.220
If you’re new to the work of Douglas Adams,
you may be wondering what made people so obsessed

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:26.220
with Hitchhiker’s.

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:30.160
Here was a show in which the basic plot was
that the Earth was destroyed, and now the

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:34.800
alien guidebook researcher Ford and his human
friend Arthur were stuck traipsing around

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:35.800
the galaxy.

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:40.320
But the genius of Hitchhiker’s doesn’t
lie in the plot, but in the comic concepts

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:45.029
the show carelessly threw out by the dozens,
each clever enough to make a series in its

00:13:45.029 --> 00:13:46.029
own right.

00:13:46.029 --> 00:13:49.430
There’s the supercomputer Deep Thought,
which spends centuries calculating the meaning

00:13:49.430 --> 00:13:53.970
of “life, the universe, and everything,”
only to solemnly report that the answer is

00:13:53.970 --> 00:13:54.970
42.

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:58.580
There’s the android Marvin, who is created
with a brain so big he can conceive of the

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:03.030
futility of his existence and so spends the
series in a state of terminal depression.

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:07.020
There are time travelling Chesterfield sofas;
a bowl of petunias that’s last thought before

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:11.740
plummeting out of space and shattering on
a planet is “oh no, not again”; and a

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:15.120
spaceship crewed entirely by the most useless
members of society.

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:19.130
Yep, that last one is the same idea Adams
pitched to Doctor Who.

00:14:19.130 --> 00:14:23.450
Cannibalizing his own work would soon become
a core part of Adams’ method.

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:26.380
By April, 1978, Hitchhiker’s was a British
sensation.

00:14:26.380 --> 00:14:30.839
A book was swiftly commissioned for Adams
to write, along with a second radio series.

00:14:30.839 --> 00:14:33.480
It was enough work for any aspiring writer.

00:14:33.480 --> 00:14:35.830
But Adams wasn’t just any aspiring writer.

00:14:35.830 --> 00:14:39.370
He was a madman with an attraction to impossible
workloads.

00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:43.870
Which may be why Adams agreed to also take
on the job of script editing Doctor Who.

00:14:43.870 --> 00:14:48.920
Although it’s overshadowed by Hitchhiker’s,
Adams’ year at Doctor Who is semi-legendary.

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:53.140
Adams took on the job out of a fondness of
the show, and a mistaken belief that it wouldn’t

00:14:53.140 --> 00:14:54.880
be too demanding.

00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:59.020
Instead he found himself working on a show
where veteran writers would write a four page

00:14:59.020 --> 00:15:03.220
treatment for a four part serial, hand it
to the script editor and tell him to turn

00:15:03.220 --> 00:15:04.860
it into two hours of television.

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:09.150
This meant Adams doing the work not only of
ten people, but also handling rewrites on

00:15:09.150 --> 00:15:10.250
top of that.

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:14.130
But it also meant a year of Doctor Who filled
with Hitchhiker’s-style inspiration.

00:15:14.130 --> 00:15:19.640
The highlight of this was City of Death, a
four part story set in Paris where the Doctor

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:25.649
steals the Mona Lisa, meets Leonardo da Vinci,
shares a scene with John Cleese, and accidentally

00:15:25.649 --> 00:15:27.230
creates all life on Earth.

00:15:27.230 --> 00:15:32.670
It’s a fun, joyful, hilarious bit of TV,
and it was written in a single, panic-and-alcohol

00:15:32.670 --> 00:15:38.190
fueled weekend when the original writer dropped
out at the last second, leaving Adams to pick

00:15:38.190 --> 00:15:39.570
up the pieces.

00:15:39.570 --> 00:15:43.820
Incredible as City of Death was, though, it
severely restricted Adams’ time for working

00:15:43.820 --> 00:15:45.040
on Hitchhiker’s.

00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:50.400
By early 1979, Adams was in a catastrophic
state of procrastination on the book adaptation,

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:52.170
with almost nothing written.

00:15:52.170 --> 00:15:56.610
Finally, as the deadline swept by, his editor
just told him to finish whatever page he was

00:15:56.610 --> 00:15:59.110
on and hand the damn thing over.

00:15:59.110 --> 00:16:03.829
With a third of the original season still
unadapted, Adams meekly complied.

00:16:03.829 --> 00:16:07.110
Book one of the Hitchhiker’s trilogy was
published in September, 1979.

00:16:07.110 --> 00:16:10.070
It immediately shot to the top of the bestseller
lists.

00:16:10.070 --> 00:16:14.220
Aged only 27, Adams was now at the head of
a bona fide phenomenon.

00:16:14.220 --> 00:16:19.570
Now all he needed to do was make sure it didn’t
go to his head.

00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:26.450
God and Nature
The 1980s began in a whirlwind of success

00:16:26.450 --> 00:16:27.450
for Adams.

00:16:27.450 --> 00:16:31.709
As the rest of Britain embraced the Thatcher
era - reluctantly or otherwise - Hitchhiker’s

00:16:31.709 --> 00:16:36.150
season two went out on radio, followed by
the second book, The Restaurant at the End

00:16:36.150 --> 00:16:40.390
of the Universe, and then a BAFTA-winning
BBC TV adaptation.

00:16:40.390 --> 00:16:45.500
With all this exposure came what every struggling
writer always craves.

00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:46.550
Money.

00:16:46.550 --> 00:16:48.380
Lots and lots of money.

00:16:48.380 --> 00:16:51.269
By 1981, Adams was a seriously wealthy guy.

00:16:51.269 --> 00:16:55.860
He began channeling his father’s spirit,
blowing cash on cars, girls, meals, music,

00:16:55.860 --> 00:16:56.860
and computers.

00:16:56.860 --> 00:16:57.860
Yes, computers.

00:16:57.860 --> 00:17:01.620
One of the reasons companies like Google program
Hitchhiker’s easter eggs into their search

00:17:01.620 --> 00:17:06.399
engine is because Adams was a hugely influential
writer on technology.

00:17:06.399 --> 00:17:10.079
He was one of the first to grasp what the
coming information age would mean, and toured

00:17:10.079 --> 00:17:13.209
the US giving witty, knowledgeable lectures
on the subject.

00:17:13.209 --> 00:17:17.889
But while this won him legions of fans in
Silicon Valley, writing was still his bread

00:17:17.889 --> 00:17:18.889
and butter.

00:17:18.889 --> 00:17:22.499
And Adams was starting to find it harder and
harder.

00:17:22.499 --> 00:17:26.379
When the time came to write the third Hitchhiker’s
book, Adams simply cannibalized one of his

00:17:26.379 --> 00:17:28.820
old, unproduced scripts for Doctor Who.

00:17:28.820 --> 00:17:34.370
And so it was that Doctor Who and the Krikket
Men became Life, the Universe and Everything.

00:17:34.370 --> 00:17:39.539
By the time the fourth book in the increasingly
inaccurately-named trilogy came out, in 1984,

00:17:39.539 --> 00:17:42.530
Adams could barely stand to be with his own
creation.

00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:46.730
“To be honest, I really shouldn't have written
[it],” he later said, “and I felt that

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:47.820
when I was writing it.

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:52.299
I did the best I could, but it wasn't, you
know, really from the heart.”

00:17:52.299 --> 00:17:56.740
But even as his chronic procrastination began
to turn into fully-fledged writer’s block,

00:17:56.740 --> 00:17:58.760
Adams’s luck kept right on going.

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:02.950
At the start of the decade, he met his future
wife, Jane Elizabeth Belson.

00:18:02.950 --> 00:18:05.600
He also met an absurd number of famous people.

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:09.350
Before long, Adams and Belsons’s house in
Ilsington was throwing parties where you could

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:14.570
bump into anyone from John Cleese, to Stephen
Fry, to Richard Dawkins.

00:18:14.570 --> 00:18:16.799
Yes, that Richard Dawkins.

00:18:16.799 --> 00:18:22.140
Adams described himself as a “radical atheist,”
and was naturally besties with the scientist.

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:25.619
But perhaps the coolest people Adams befriended
were Pink Floyd.

00:18:25.619 --> 00:18:29.330
There are legends that, halfway through his
parties, Adams would hand out guitars, and

00:18:29.330 --> 00:18:32.190
treat the crowd to an impromptu Pink Floyd
gig.

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:37.239
But even as Adams’ star rose, he was beginning
to discover just how ephemeral this shiny

00:18:37.239 --> 00:18:38.340
world was.

00:18:38.340 --> 00:18:43.549
In 1985, Adams was sent alongside zoologist
Mark Carwardine to Madagascar to write an

00:18:43.549 --> 00:18:46.529
article about endangered animals for the Observer.

00:18:46.529 --> 00:18:51.120
The two hit it off and, a couple of years
later, did a radio series together about endangered

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:52.120
species.

00:18:52.120 --> 00:18:54.580
It was a show that would change Adams’ life.

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:58.619
The show and it’s book Last Chance to See
was Adams’ personal wake-up call to the

00:18:58.619 --> 00:19:01.250
damage humans were doing to their environment.

00:19:01.250 --> 00:19:05.539
In the aftermath, he would become one of the
earliest, loudest voices on conservation and

00:19:05.539 --> 00:19:07.779
the dangers posed by habitat destruction.

00:19:07.779 --> 00:19:10.720
Still, the 1980s ended on a relatively good
note.

00:19:10.720 --> 00:19:14.799
Adams managed to get over his writer’s block
long enough to write two Dirk Gently detective

00:19:14.799 --> 00:19:20.669
stories (one of which, again, cannibalized
his old Doctor Who scripts) and, in 1991,

00:19:20.669 --> 00:19:22.690
finally married Jane Elizabeth Belson.

00:19:22.690 --> 00:19:27.840
At the start of the 90s, Adams was in his
late thirties, world famous, rich, and married

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:28.990
to the woman of his dreams.

00:19:28.990 --> 00:19:32.730
If this kept up, who knew what the rest of
his life would be like?

00:19:32.730 --> 00:19:36.330
Unfortunately, we here in the future already
know the tragic answer.

00:19:36.330 --> 00:19:40.960
Douglas Adams may have not yet been forty…
…but he was already entering the final decade

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:42.749
of his life.

00:19:42.749 --> 00:19:50.289
So Long, and Thanks for All the Fish
The last ten years of Adams’ life dawned

00:19:50.289 --> 00:19:51.910
with the writer in a bleak place.

00:19:51.910 --> 00:19:55.509
After nearly seven years, he’d agreed to
write the fifth Hitchhiker’s book, on the

00:19:55.509 --> 00:19:57.850
understanding that it would be the last.

00:19:57.850 --> 00:19:59.950
Even so, his writer’s block crippled him.

00:19:59.950 --> 00:20:02.229
There would be nothing, for weeks on end.

00:20:02.229 --> 00:20:06.520
Nothing but Adams locked away in hotels by
his grim-faced editor, staring at the ceiling.

00:20:06.520 --> 00:20:10.739
We’ve heard tales that the publisher hired
a fleet of writers to sit downstairs, churning

00:20:10.739 --> 00:20:16.320
out chapters based on Adams’ outline, until
Adams was finally roused to work by the thought

00:20:16.320 --> 00:20:19.210
of all these other writers doing his book
wrong.

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:21.539
Mostly Harmless was released in 1992.

00:20:21.539 --> 00:20:26.239
It was, without a doubt, the bleakest thing
Adams ever wrote, with a downer ending that

00:20:26.239 --> 00:20:28.049
leaves a nasty taste in the mouth.

00:20:28.049 --> 00:20:32.009
Much later, Adams would claim he’d been
seriously depressed while writing it.

00:20:32.009 --> 00:20:33.510
It really shows.

00:20:33.510 --> 00:20:38.549
Still, life moves fast and, within a couple
of years of the gloomy conclusion of Hitchhiker’s,

00:20:38.549 --> 00:20:40.960
Adams seemed to be living life to the full
again.

00:20:40.960 --> 00:20:46.359
For his 42nd birthday in 1994, for example,
he was invited onstage by Pink Floyd to play

00:20:46.359 --> 00:20:47.409
a gig with them.

00:20:47.409 --> 00:20:50.690
Later that same year, his daughter, Polly
Jane Rocket Adams, was born.

00:20:50.690 --> 00:20:55.179
By the 21st Century, Adams was lecturing in
Silicon Valley, developing video games with

00:20:55.179 --> 00:20:59.419
his production company, and working on turning
Hitchhiker’s into a Hollywood movie.

00:20:59.419 --> 00:21:04.029
He’d even started writing again, tentatively
putting together ten chapters of a third Dirk

00:21:04.029 --> 00:21:08.629
Gently book that he thought might even become
the sixth Hitchhiker’s book.

00:21:08.629 --> 00:21:09.779
And then it happened.

00:21:09.779 --> 00:21:10.779
The moment.

00:21:10.779 --> 00:21:15.239
The moment as shocking to his fandom as the
sight of a Vogon Constructor Fleet hanging

00:21:15.239 --> 00:21:18.909
in the air in much the same way that bricks
don't.

00:21:18.909 --> 00:21:23.229
On May 11, 2001, Douglas Adams suffered a
massive heart attack at his local gym.

00:21:23.229 --> 00:21:26.200
At the time he died, he wasn’t even 50.

00:21:26.200 --> 00:21:29.450
In the wake of Adams’ death, tributes poured
in from around the world.

00:21:29.450 --> 00:21:33.779
This wasn’t just the regular sadness we
feel when someone passes away.

00:21:33.779 --> 00:21:38.820
This was a keenly felt pain, almost personal
- like that felt when John Lennon or David

00:21:38.820 --> 00:21:39.820
Bowie died.

00:21:39.820 --> 00:21:44.609
As Stephen Fry later noted, Hitchhiker’s
had been sci-fi on a human scale.

00:21:44.609 --> 00:21:49.630
It resonated with people, made it feel like
Adams was talking to them and them alone.

00:21:49.630 --> 00:21:53.479
And that may be why his death devastated so
many people.

00:21:53.479 --> 00:21:56.359
But what should we today make of Douglas Adams?

00:21:56.359 --> 00:22:01.479
What should we make of the man who wrote a
handful of slim, comic volumes before vanishing

00:22:01.479 --> 00:22:02.479
into the abyss?

00:22:02.479 --> 00:22:03.809
Well, it depends on your perspective.

00:22:03.809 --> 00:22:08.659
Right now, it’s still too early to tell
what Adams’s long term impact on fiction

00:22:08.659 --> 00:22:09.659
will be.

00:22:09.659 --> 00:22:14.549
While he has endless admirers, there’s a
sense that his work was maybe too unique,

00:22:14.549 --> 00:22:19.389
a dead end for any writer who doesn’t happen
to be a comic genius.

00:22:19.389 --> 00:22:22.580
On the other hand, Adams’ influence is already
everywhere.

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:26.929
We mentioned earlier that Adams was an early
adopter - of environmentalism, of computers,

00:22:26.929 --> 00:22:32.360
of ideas - and part of his charm was his ability
to take those ideas and explain them simply,

00:22:32.360 --> 00:22:35.809
in ways that still impact the ways his readers
think.

00:22:35.809 --> 00:22:40.570
As Neil Gaiman said in 2015:
“I think perhaps what Douglas was, was a

00:22:40.570 --> 00:22:42.679
futurologist, or an explainer.

00:22:42.679 --> 00:22:46.919
One day maybe we’ll realise that the most
important job there is, is someone who can

00:22:46.919 --> 00:22:51.530
explain the world to itself in ways the world
can’t forget.”

00:22:51.530 --> 00:22:56.140
The continents may grow old and die, the world
may be demolished to make way for a hyperspace

00:22:56.140 --> 00:23:02.609
bypass, but somewhere, out there in the cosmos,
some lifeform will always be sat in a restaurant

00:23:02.609 --> 00:23:08.119
at the end of the universe, reading in wide-eyed
wonder as Douglas Adams explains the world

00:23:08.119 --> 00:23:09.169
to it.

00:23:09.169 --> 00:23:23.329
And that lifeform will be smiling.

